# P25K_G's CBIOS and PBIOS - the older PHILIPS P2000B generation, and the
# only sample of it this project has. It is also the disk with the most
# software on it, which is why it is worth naming.
#
#   ./p2500-emu --disk disks/P25K_G.raw --symbols symbols/p25k_g.sym \
#     --max-steps 3000000 --disasm EDDC:12
#   p2500-gui --disk disks/P25K_G.raw --symbols symbols/p25k_g.sym --panels disasm
#
# NOT symbols/cpm.sym plus an offset. Both SYSCBI.PHI files load at $E200,
# but G's ends at $E833 where P25K_B's ends at $EABE, and its CBIOS is a
# different implementation - the two diverge at the first instruction of
# CONIN. Its SYSPBI.PHI loads at $EB80 against B's $EAC0, the +$C0 the
# research notes record, but inside that module the routines have drifted
# by their own amounts: +$AF, +$BB, +$83, +$31, and +$90 all appear below.
#
# How each name here was established, so it can be re-checked or refuted:
#
#   - the driver layer, from G's own device table and function tables,
#     matched to B's by device id and function code (tools/sym_check.py's
#     companion, tools/port_syms.py --devtable, in the research repo)
#   - the interrupt handlers, from G's IM2 trampolines, which are the only
#     place those addresses appear
#   - the rest of PBIOS, by walking both builds' disassembly in lockstep
#     from those entry points: every name below had at least two
#     instructions whose operands say so, or was confirmed by hand
#   - the CBIOS half, from G's own SELDSK, CONIN and CONOUT bodies
#   - three tables, by content: the baud table and the sector-translate
#     table are byte-identical to B's, and the escape table has the same
#     {code, pointer} records
#
# Four names in cpm.sym have NO counterpart here, which is a finding, not
# a gap in this file: TICKS (G's clock ISR has no counter block at all -
# so nothing in RAM ticks at 50 Hz on this disk, and the field-sync trick
# in demos/p2500.inc does not work), KEYXLAT.TAB (no key translation
# table - no cursor diamond, no dead keys), ESC.COUNT and CONOUT.XLAT
# (G's CONOUT only posts a request; the screen driver owns the escapes).
#
# Fingerprint, so `--symbols auto` can recognise this build without
# being told which disk is in the drive:
# the same window over P25K_G's own, different, CBIOS build.
# match E200:0400:E2D1C08D
#
# And the disk itself, recognisable before anything has booted - the CP/M
# directory at $2000. See symbols/cpm.sym for why it has to be the
# directory rather than the boot track.
# match-disk 2000:0800:ECEBB62C P25K_G - PHILIPS P2000B games disk
#
# Format: ADDR NAME, hex, one per line. $ or 0x prefix optional, : or =
# accepted in place of the space, # and ; start a comment.

# ---- CP/M itself: the same Digital Research build as every other disk
#      here, byte-identical where it is code
CC00 CCP
D400 BDOS.base
E086 BDOS.RESET
E117 BDOS.RDROVEC
E1AD RO.VECTOR
E1AF LOGIN.VECTOR
E1C1 BDOS.DPBCOPY   ; BDOS's own copy of the DPB - at $E1C1 in both builds

# ---- CBIOS: disk, from G's SELDSK ($E469: drive*16 + $E233)
E233 DPH.TABLE      ; stride 16; DPH[0] = XLT $E253, DIRBUF, DPB, CSV, ALV
E253 SECTRAN.TAB    ; byte-identical to B's: 01 02 05 06 09 0A 0D 0E ...
E273 DPB            ; SPT 32, BSH/BLM 4/15, EXM 1, DSM 149, DRM 63,
                    ; CKS 16, OFF 2 - and AL0 $C0 where B has $80
E291 DRIVE.MAP      ; two entries ($10 $10): this BIOS knows two drives
E2A7 DRIVEMAP.PTR   ; SELDSK reads the map through this pointer
E74B DRIVE.CUR      ; SELDSK stores the selected drive here
E74C TRACK.CUR      ; SETTRK stores the track here
E76B DIRBUF         ; from DPH[0]
E7FF ALV.A          ; from DPH[0]
E823 CSV.A          ; from DPH[0]

# ---- CBIOS: console
E2AF REQ.SUBMIT     ; one instruction, jp REQ.POST - CBIOS's door into PBIOS
E390 CONIN.body     ; also derived live; here for when page zero is not CP/M's
E3A8 CONIN.MASK     ; ld a,(CAPS.MASK) / and b
E3AD CAPS.CHECK     ; polls KBD.REQ's status, then toggles the mask
E3B9 CAPS.TOGGLE    ; xor $20 into CAPS.MASK
E3C3 CAPS.MASK      ; $DF as shipped, and ANDed with the input - so this
                    ; BIOS CLAMPS to upper case where B's XOR $20 SWAPS
                    ; case. Verified by typing: `DIR` echoes `DIR` here
                    ; and `dir` on P25K_B
E3C4 KBD.REQ        ; the console-in request block
E3C6 KBD.REQ.BUSY   ; CONIN spins on this before posting
E3C8 KBD.REQ.STAT   ; and waits for $80 here
E3C9 KBD.REQ2       ; a second block for device $30, posted by BOOT

# ---- PBIOS: the request and driver layer
EB80 REQ.POST       ; B $EAC0 +$C0 - post a request, then spin on its status
EC16 DEV.TABLE      ; count byte, then {id, ptr_lo, ptr_hi}; ids $31 $30
                    ; $00 $01 $40 $FF - two disk units, where B has four
EC46 DRV.DISPATCH   ; every driver's `ld hl,fntab / call here`
EC9D EI.RETI        ; how every handler releases the daisy chain
ECA0 RING.PUSH      ; circular buffer push
ECB6 RING.POP       ; and pop
ECE6 DEVFF.DRV      ; device $FF, purpose unidentified (B $EC2B)
ECFB DEVFF.FNTAB

# ---- PBIOS: keyboard (device $30)
ED5A KBD.DRV
ED76 KBD.FNTAB      ; f00 $ED84, f01 $EDB1, f03 $EDBE, f04 $EDCB
EDB1 KBD.RINGINIT
EDD9 CTC3.ISR       ; keyboard byte-ready, from the IM2 trampoline at $FF2A
EDDC CTC3.ISR.body  ; in a,($06) at $EDE7
EE38 KBD.REQ.PTR    ; the driver's current request
EE3E KBD.RING       ; header [count][write][read], 32 bytes at +3

# ---- PBIOS: screen (device $31)
EE84 SCREEN.DRV
EEAB SCREEN.FNTAB   ; f00 $EEB9, f01 $EEDE, f03 $EEF7, f05 $EF1D
EF1D SCREEN.WRITE   ; function $05
F1FB ESC.TAB        ; {code, pointer} records, same shape as B's
F224 ESC.TAB2       ; the alternate set
F4B6 SCREEN.REQ.PTR
F4B8 SCREEN.FLAGS   ; bit 2 drives the generated-code queue below
F4BE ATTR.CUR       ; current attribute; its low nibble reaches port $0A
F4D7 ATTR.TMPL      ; 3E 00 D3 0A - the OUT ($0A) template
F4EF CRTC.R10TMPL   ; the R10 write template used by ESC C / ESC c
F4F8 GENQ.WPTR      ; write pointer into the generated-code queue
FD54 GENQ.BUF       ; the queue itself, terminated $C9 and then called

# ---- PBIOS: clock
F40F CTC2.ISR       ; 50 Hz field strobe, from the trampoline at $FF33
F412 CTC2.ISR.body  ; NO tick counter here - see the header

# ---- PBIOS: serial / printer (device $40)
F53C SER.DRV
F558 SER.FNTAB      ; f00 $F569, f01 $F5BC, f03 $F5D7, f04 $F60C, f05 $F5EB
F5C9 BAUD.TAB       ; 7 x {control, time constant}, byte-identical to B's
F61A CTC0.ISR       ; transmit clock
F629 TX.WAIT        ; waits on port $05 bit 6
F6EC CTC1.ISR       ; receive sampler
F7AD TX.FLAGS
F7B0 TX.TIMEOUT
F7B2 SER.REQ.PTR

# ---- PBIOS: disk (devices $00 and $01)
F82A DISK.DRV
F858 DISK.FNTAB     ; nine functions, same codes as B's
FBCC DMA.ISR        ; from the trampoline at $FF21
FC4C PIOA.ISR       ; from the trampoline at $FF18

# ---- The IM2 layer, at I=$FF
FF18 IM2.TRAMP      ; six trampolines, $FF18 to $FF45, vector order
FF63 ISR.SHIM       ; saves A, patches its own jp with the handler address
FFC0 CBIOS.IM2      ; vectors $C0-$CB: CTC 0-3, PIO A, DMA
FFDE ISR.SAVE.A
FFDF ISR.SAVE.HL

# ---- Derived straight from this machine's own tables by
#      tools/derive_syms.py (research repo), which needs no
#      reference build: the per-drive check and allocation
#      vectors out of each DPH, and every driver's function
#      entries out of its function table. Function codes are
#      the driver's own; $05 is write on the screen driver.
E7EB ALV.B
E813 CSV.B
ED03 DEVFF.F00
ED3E DEVFF.F03
ED84 KBD.F00
EDBE KBD.F03
EDCB KBD.F04
EEB9 SCREEN.F00
EEDE SCREEN.F01
EEF7 SCREEN.F03
F569 SER.F00
F5BC SER.F01
F5D7 SER.F03
F5EB SER.F05
F60C SER.F04
F875 DISK.F00
F8BB DISK.F01
F8C3 DISK.F02
F8F6 DISK.F03
F91B DISK.F06
F923 DISK.F07
F92F DISK.F04
F93E DISK.F05
F94D DISK.F08
